course: history
lesson: 60
title: China: Mei's exaggerations and everyday China
level: primary-4
stars: 3
explain: Mei says China invented everything: paper, football, ice cream. Danylo says Ukraine is the oldest and greatest. The history teacher smiles, because every country tells its story with pride, and history class is where the proud stories get checked, kindly.
explain: Some of Mei's claims hold: paper, printing, the compass, gunpowder and silk really came from China, FIFA calls the ancient game cuju the earliest known form of football, and wild giant pandas live only in China. Some are stretched: zero came from India, modern football rules from England, and «the whole world» does not celebrate the Lunar New Year.
explain: Think of a school sports day: each class cheers for its own team, but the stopwatch is the same for everyone. Sources are history's stopwatch. Tu Youyou used one: she tested an old Chinese remedy and found a malaria medicine that won a Nobel Prize.
explain: That closes the course. Mei and Danylo learnt to check their sources; the teacher said he learnt a lot from both of them. Pride and truth can live together, and the best history is the one that survives the check.
fact: China invented many things, including paper, printing, the compass, gunpowder, porcelain, silk, paper money and the seismoscope; but not everything: the alphabet came from the Middle East, the number zero from India and the working steam engine from Britain.
fact: FIFA recognises cuju, a kicking-ball game from ancient China, as the earliest known form of football; the rules of modern football were written in England in 1863.
fact: People in Tang China kept ice and made frozen milk desserts; Persians and Arabs also made frozen sweets, so ice cream has several early homes and no clear first.
fact: The Lunar New Year, or Spring Festival, is celebrated in China, Vietnam and Korea and by Chinese communities around the world, with dragon and lion dances, fireworks and red envelopes holding money.
fact: The Chinese zodiac counts years in a 12-year cycle of animals: rat, ox, tiger, rabbit, dragon, snake, horse, goat, monkey, rooster, dog and pig; the idea that your animal decides your character is tradition and fun, not science.
fact: Chinese has thousands of characters, and Mandarin has four tones, where the pitch of a syllable changes its meaning; it is hard for English speakers, but children in China learn it like any language.
fact: Mandarin has the most native speakers in the world; English has the most speakers in total when second-language learners are counted.
fact: The Shaolin Temple in Henan province, about 1,500 years old, is famous for kung fu; monks give demonstrations with hard training and some showmanship, but nobody flies, that is film magic.
fact: Wild giant pandas live only in the mountains of China; since the 1980s zoos abroad usually rent pandas from China instead of receiving them as gifts, and cubs born abroad are usually sent to China.
fact: In Chinese tradition the dragon, «long», brings rain and good fortune and is a symbol of power, while European dragons are often villains; Chinese legends have some dangerous dragons too.
fact: Traditional Chinese medicine is very old; Tu Youyou won the 2015 Nobel Prize for artemisinin, a malaria drug from sweet wormwood, a plant named in old texts, but many traditional remedies are not proven by tests and some use endangered animals.
fact: Both Ukraine and China have long, rich histories; every nation has proud myths, and checking them kindly against sources is what history class is for.
exercise: Which important number came from India? | Zero | One | Seven | A hundred
exercise: Which of these did China not invent? | The steam engine | Paper made from bark | The magnetic compass | Porcelain from fine white clay
exercise: What was the ancient Chinese ball game called? | Cuju | Kung fu | Go | Xiangqi
exercise: Where were the rules of modern football written? | In England in 1863 | In China, about 2,000 years ago | In Brazil, for the 1950 World Cup | In Italy in 1500
exercise: How did people keep ice before fridges? | In ice houses and cool pits, stored from winter | In closed ovens, where the heat could not reach it | In paper bags hung high up on the roof | In salty sea water, which never lets ice melt
exercise: Which statement about ice cream is fair? | It has several early homes, China among them | Italy alone invented it, in Marco Polo's time | China alone invented it, and nobody else did | It was first made in America around 1990
exercise: What is put in red envelopes at the Lunar New Year? | Money | Rice | Letters to the emperor | Fireworks
exercise: Which countries celebrate the Lunar New Year besides China? | Vietnam and Korea, and Chinese communities everywhere | Every country on Earth, as an official public holiday | Only Japan, which took it from China long ago | None at all: it is kept only inside China itself
exercise: How long is one cycle of the Chinese zodiac? | 12 years | 7 years | 100 years | 1 year
exercise: Which animal is in the Chinese zodiac? | The rooster | The cat | The panda | The lion
exercise: What are tones in Mandarin? | Changes of pitch that change a word's meaning | Musical instruments played at New Year | Kinds of characters written with a brush | The loud and quiet days of the Chinese week
exercise: Which language has the most native speakers? | Mandarin | English | Spanish | Ukrainian
exercise: Which language has the most speakers in total, with learners? | English | Mandarin | Latin | Hindi
exercise: Where is the Shaolin Temple? | In Henan province, China | In the old centre of Tokyo | On a green hill in Hong Kong | High in the mountains of Tibet
exercise: Why are panda cubs born abroad sent to China? | The pandas are on loan from China | The cubs are lost | Zoos abroad dislike pandas | China pays the zoos to give them away
exercise: What did Chinese dragons bring in tradition? | Rain and good fortune | Fire and ruin only | Snow and darkness | Gold coins from the sky
exercise: What did Tu Youyou discover? | Artemisinin, a malaria medicine | Penicillin, from bread mould | The compass, for sailors | Green tea as a cure for colds
exercise: What did Mei and Danylo learn in history class? | To check their sources and keep their pride | That only one country in history was ever great | That history is boring and only about old dates | That teachers always know everything already
test: Mei says China invented everything first. What would the teacher kindly say? | Many great things, yes, but zero, the alphabet and the steam engine came from elsewhere | Correct: every invention in history, from paper to the steam engine, is Chinese | China invented nothing; paper, silk and the compass all came from Europe | Only gunpowder is Chinese; paper and the compass came from India
test: The teacher says «England invented football», Mei says «China». FIFA? | Both: cuju is the ancient ancestor, England wrote the modern rules | Only England: there was no kicking-ball game in China before the 1800s | Only China: England just copied the cuju rules word for word | Neither: Brazil invented it and wrote the first rules
test: shut up mei, italy made ice cream. who's right? | Neither alone; ice cream has several early homes | Only Italy, proven, it's in all the books | Only China, proven, Marco Polo said so | Nobody, ice cream was invented in America in 2000
test: Mei says the whole world celebrates Chinese New Year. What is accurate? | Many communities around the world celebrate it | Every person on Earth celebrates it as a holiday | Nobody outside China has ever even heard of it | It is celebrated only in China and only in Beijing
test: Mei: «I'm a dragon, so I'm born to lead!» What is wise? | Enjoy the tradition; the zodiac does not decide character | Agree: science has proved that dragon people make the best leaders | Disagree: only people born in rat years can lead | The zodiac is a law of physics, so it must be true
test: The teacher said Chinese is impossible to learn. What did Mei show? | Hard is not impossible: she taught the class «nǐ hǎo» | He was right: no foreigner has ever learned to speak Chinese | Chinese has no tones, so it is in fact very easy | Chinese has only 26 letters, just like English
test: The teacher says English is the most spoken; Mei says Mandarin. Who wins? | Both, by different measures: total speakers and native speakers | Only the teacher, because English has the most native speakers by far | Only Mei, because Mandarin is first by every measure | Neither of them: Latin still has the most speakers
test: a film shows Shaolin monks flying over the roof. real? | No, flying is film magic; the training is real | Yes, they train for 20 years | Yes, but only at night | No, Shaolin monks do not exist
test: If a zoo abroad had a panda cub, what would usually happen? | It would be sent to China after some time | It would stay forever as a gift to that country | It would be released into the wild in that country | It would be sold to another zoo for a lot of money
test: A video game shows a Chinese dragon burning a village. What is unusual about that? | Chinese dragons usually bring rain and luck, not ruin | Nothing; Chinese dragons are always villains | Chinese dragons cannot move | There are no Chinese dragons
test: Mei says Chinese medicine cures everything; the teacher says «not science». What did Tu Youyou show? | Test old remedies; keep the ones that work | Every old remedy works, so no tests are needed | No old remedy can ever work, so throw them out | Tests are not needed if a remedy is very old
test: Danylo and Mei both say their country is the oldest and greatest. What did the course teach? | Pride and truth can live together when sources are checked | Only one of them can be right, so they must fight it out | History is just opinion, so any claim is as good as another | Nobody should ever be proud of their country's history
exam: Identify the game recognised by FIFA as the earliest known form of football. | Cuju, a kicking-ball game of ancient China | Xiangqi, the Chinese chess | Go, the game of stones | Kung fu, the Shaolin art
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning the discovery for which Tu Youyou was awarded the Nobel Prize in 2015. | Artemisinin, a remedy for malaria derived from sweet wormwood | Penicillin, a remedy for infections of the blood derived from bread mould | A vaccine against smallpox derived from the cowpox of cattle | Green tea, a remedy held to cure every illness of the body
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the distinction between Mandarin and English with regard to the number of speakers? | Mandarin has the most native speakers; English the most speakers in total | English has the most native speakers; Mandarin the most speakers in total | Both languages possess exactly equal numbers of speakers | Neither language is spoken by more than a million persons
exam: Ascertain the arrangement under which giant pandas are ordinarily held in zoos outside China since the 1980s. | Loans from China, with cubs usually returned to China | Permanent gifts from China to foreign kings and heads of state | Purchase at auction by the highest bidding zoo | Capture in the wild by foreign zoo expeditions
